items: f-front-exit-code-replaces-the-real-diagnostic
paths: file:v2/bin/ssc1-run-fsub.ssc0 file:v1/tools/cli/src/main/scala/scalascript/cli/RunNativeV2.scala file:tests/e2e/f-front-exit-reason-gate.sh file:scripts/smoke-ci.ssc file:tests/BUGS.md
scope: |
  TWO HALVES, and the first is why the second went unseen.

  (1) The CLI throws `native frontend exited with N` and DISCARDS the tower's captured output, so
      `ssc info --front-report` records an exit code where a diagnostic exists. That is the one
      census bucket that names no mechanism.
  (2) Behind it, one mechanism for all five files: F's runner has an ordering pass the reference
      front does not have at all (sscOrderRoot, ssc1-run-fsub.ssc0:924), and it calls #io.exit(1)
      when a root has no `scalascript` fences. sscLoadMod, three lines up and IDENTICAL in both
      runners, keeps the stderr note and continues with an empty statement list — its comment says
      why: "Short-circuiting to an empty program instead only trades `exit 1` for an ABI error."

  Subjects: examples/deploy.ssc, std/graphql.ssc, std/index.ssc and the two frontend dashboards are
  documentation modules whose only fences are ```sh / ```text. The reference front runs them as an
  empty program; F exits 1.

  Of F's three #io.exit sites the other two are usage errors unreachable from front-report, so
  fixing this one removes the observable class — which also means the CLI half will have no
  reachable subject to gate with afterwards, and is guarded by reading rather than by a row.
next: gate red on the five doc-only files, fix sscOrderRoot, carry the tower text in the CLI message
